# Property and Equipment Management Policy

**{{ org_name }}** — a 501(c)(3) nonprofit research institute incorporated in {{ state }}
EIN: {{ ein }}

## Article I — Purpose and Scope

This policy establishes standards for the management, control, and disposition of property and equipment acquired by {{ org_name }} ("the Organization") with federal and non-federal funds. It is designed to ensure compliance with the Uniform Guidance (2 CFR 200.310–316) and to protect the Organization's assets and the interests of federal awarding agencies.

### 1.1 Definitions

- **Equipment**: Tangible personal property (including information technology systems) with a useful life of more than one year and a per-unit acquisition cost of **$10,000 or more** (per 2 CFR 200.1), unless a specific award or sponsor imposes a lower threshold.
- **Supplies**: All tangible personal property other than equipment — generally items with an acquisition cost below $10,000 or a useful life of one year or less.
- **Computing devices**: Machines used for acquiring, storing, analyzing, processing, and publishing data and other information electronically (desktops, laptops, tablets, and similar devices).
- **Intangible property**: Non-physical assets such as copyrights, patents, trademarks, and software licenses.

### 1.2 Applicability

This policy applies to all directors, officers, employees, and agents of the Organization who acquire, use, maintain, or dispose of property and equipment on behalf of the Organization, regardless of funding source. Federally funded equipment is additionally subject to the conditions of the specific award under which it was acquired.

## Article II — Property Records

The Organization shall maintain property records for all equipment that include, at minimum:

1. Description of the equipment (manufacturer, model, specifications);
2. Serial number or other identification number;
3. Source of funding, including the federal award identification number (FAIN) for federally purchased items;
4. Title holder (the Organization, the federal government, or shared);
5. Acquisition date and unit acquisition cost;
6. Current condition (excellent, good, fair, poor, or salvage);
7. Physical location of the equipment;
8. Percentage of federal participation in the acquisition cost; and
9. Ultimate disposition data, including the date of disposal and sale price (or method of disposal).

The Executive Director (or designee) shall maintain these records in an equipment inventory register. Records shall be retained for three years after final disposition of the equipment, consistent with the Organization's Document Retention Policy and 2 CFR 200.334.

## Article III — Physical Inventory

### 3.1 Frequency

A physical inventory of all equipment shall be conducted **at least once every two years**. The inventory must verify the existence, current condition, and location of each item against the property records.

### 3.2 Procedure

The Executive Director (or designee) shall:

- Compare each item on the inventory register to its physical location;
- Note any discrepancies (missing items, condition changes, location changes);
- Investigate and resolve all discrepancies within 60 days; and
- Report findings to the Board of Directors at the next regular meeting.

### 3.3 Reconciliation

Any items that cannot be located during the physical inventory shall be investigated. If an item is confirmed lost, stolen, or destroyed, the Organization shall follow the procedures in Article IV and shall notify the federal awarding agency if the item was federally funded and the loss exceeds $10,000 or if notice is otherwise required by the award terms.

## Article IV — Control and Safeguards

### 4.1 General Controls

The Organization shall implement adequate safeguards to prevent loss, damage, or theft of all property and equipment:

- Equipment shall be stored in secure, climate-appropriate spaces;
- Access to high-value equipment (acquisition cost exceeding $10,000) shall be limited to authorized personnel;
- Portable equipment (laptops, tablets, field instruments) issued to individuals shall be documented with a signed equipment checkout form;
- All equipment shall be labeled with an Organization asset tag upon receipt.

### 4.2 Insurance

The Organization shall maintain adequate insurance coverage for equipment against loss, damage, and theft, including coverage for equipment in transit or at off-site locations. Coverage amounts and terms shall be reviewed annually.

### 4.3 Reporting Loss or Damage

Any employee who discovers loss, damage, or theft of Organization equipment shall report it to the Executive Director within 24 hours. The Executive Director shall document the incident, file an insurance claim if appropriate, and notify the federal awarding agency if the equipment was federally funded.

## Article V — Maintenance

### 5.1 Preventive Maintenance

The Organization shall maintain equipment in good working condition. For equipment with manufacturer-recommended maintenance schedules (e.g., laboratory instruments, servers), the Executive Director (or designee) shall establish and follow a preventive maintenance calendar.

### 5.2 Repairs

Repairs shall be documented in the property record for the affected item. If the cost of repair exceeds 50% of the current fair market value of the equipment, the Executive Director shall evaluate whether replacement is more cost-effective and shall document the analysis.

### 5.3 Computing Equipment

Computing devices shall be maintained with current operating system patches, security updates, and antivirus software. Decommissioned computing devices shall have all data securely wiped before disposal, consistent with the Organization's Data Management Policy.

## Article VI — Use of Equipment

### 6.1 Federally Funded Equipment

Equipment acquired with federal funds shall be used in the program or project for which it was acquired as long as needed, whether or not the project continues to be supported by the federal award. When no longer needed for the original project, the equipment shall be made available for use on other federally funded activities in the following priority order:

1. Other projects funded by the same federal agency;
2. Projects funded by other federal agencies; then
3. Non-federally funded activities of the Organization.

### 6.2 Computing Devices as Direct Costs

Computing devices may be charged as a direct cost to a federal award when the device is **essential and allocable** to the project and the device is **primarily used** in the actual conduct of the funded research (per 2 CFR 200.453). Documentation supporting the allocation must be maintained.

### 6.3 Third-Party Use

The Organization shall not loan, lease, or otherwise make federally funded equipment available to third parties without prior written approval from the federal awarding agency, unless such use is incidental and does not interfere with the project for which the equipment was acquired.

## Article VII — Disposition of Equipment

### 7.1 When Equipment Is No Longer Needed

When federally funded equipment with a current per-unit fair market value exceeding **$10,000** is no longer needed for the original project or other federally funded activities, the Organization shall request disposition instructions from the federal awarding agency. The agency may direct the Organization to:

1. **Transfer** the equipment to the federal government or a third party designated by the agency;
2. **Retain** the equipment, in which case the Organization shall compensate the federal government for its share (calculated as the percentage of federal participation in the original acquisition cost, applied to the current fair market value); or
3. **Sell** the equipment and compensate the federal government for its share.

Equipment with a current per-unit fair market value of $10,000 or less may be retained, sold, or otherwise disposed of with no further obligation to the federal government.

### 7.2 Non-Federally Funded Equipment

Equipment purchased with non-federal funds may be disposed of by sale, donation, recycling, or disposal at the discretion of the Executive Director, with Board approval for items originally valued above $10,000.

### 7.3 Disposition Records

All dispositions shall be documented in the property record, including the date, method of disposition, sale price (if any), and the identity of the recipient.

## Article VIII — Supplies

Title to supplies acquired with federal funds vests in the Organization upon acquisition. If there is a **residual inventory of unused supplies exceeding $10,000 in total aggregate value** at the end of the federal award, the Organization shall compensate the federal government for its share, or use the supplies on other federal awards.

Supplies shall be stored securely and tracked with sufficient detail to ensure they are used for authorized purposes.

## Article IX — Intangible Property

Copyrights, patents, inventions, and other intangible property acquired or developed under a federal award are subject to the specific terms of the award and 37 CFR Part 401 (Bayh-Dole Act) where applicable. The Organization's Intellectual Property Policy governs internal procedures for identification, disclosure, and management of intangible property. This policy and the IP Policy shall be read together; in the event of a conflict with a specific federal award term, the award term controls.

The federal government retains, at minimum, a royalty-free, nonexclusive, and irrevocable right to reproduce, publish, or otherwise use — and to authorize others to use — any work developed under a federal award, for federal purposes.

## Article X — Approval Authority and Annual Review

### 10.1 Approval Authority

| Action                             | Required Approval              |
|------------------------------------|--------------------------------|
| Equipment acquisition (any source) | Per Procurement Policy thresholds |
| Equipment checkout to individual   | Executive Director or designee |
| Disposition (FMV ≤ $10,000)        | Executive Director             |
| Disposition (FMV > $10,000)        | Executive Director + Board Chair |
| Federal disposition request        | Executive Director             |

### 10.2 Annual Review

The Board of Directors shall review this policy annually. The Executive Director shall report on the status of the equipment inventory, any losses or dispositions during the year, and any changes in federal requirements. This review may take place at any regular or special board meeting and shall be noted in the meeting minutes.
